<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pan Africanist Congress of Azania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pan Africanist Congress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pan Africanist Congress (PAC) is a South African political party founded in 1959 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-sobukwe">Robert Sobukwe</a> and others who broke from the ANC to pursue an Africanist programme. Its campaign against the pass laws led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a> of March 1960, after which it was banned. It was unbanned in February 1990, and its armed wing merged into the national army in 1994.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The PAC was launched on 6 April 1959 at Orlando Communal Hall in Soweto. Its founders objected to the replacement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>'s 1949 Programme of Action by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a> of 1955, with its multiracialist language and its claim that the land belongs to all who live in it, both white and black. They held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> belonged to its indigenous African people by inalienable right and called for the complete freedom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Robert Sobukwe was elected the first president and Potlako Leballo secretary general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 21 March 1960 the PAC led a campaign against the pass laws, and crowds gathered in the townships of Sharpeville and Langa. Sobukwe and other leaders were arrested and convicted of incitement: Sobukwe was sentenced to three years and Leballo to two. Right after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> government banned both the ANC and the PAC on 8 April 1960, and the PAC responded by founding an armed wing, the Azanian People's Liberation Army. Sobukwe died of lung cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> in 1978.</p><p>By the 1990s the party had toned down its socialist elements in favour of a more conservative programme, which the Pan Africanist Youth Congress of Azania denounced, and by April 1992 its leadership no longer opposed joining the multiracial negotiations to end apartheid. Clarence Makwetu, who led it in the 1994 elections, was removed in 1996. Rival factions then fought repeatedly over the leadership in congresses and in the courts; in 2019 the Independent Electoral Commission recognised Mzwanele Nyhontso, and in 2024, as the party's only elected member, he joined the Government of National Unity as Minister of Land Reform and Rural Development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The PAC called its ideas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>, resting on three principles: African nationalism, socialism and continental unity. They drew on the teachings of Anton Lembede, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>, Martin Delany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>. In his inaugural speech <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-sobukwe">Sobukwe</a> described the aim as government of the Africans by the Africans, for the Africans, counting as African everyone who owed loyalty only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and accepted African majority rule. The party historically rejected Marxism and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, though it borrowed some Maoist tenets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pan Africanist Congress of Azania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1960 had been followed by a state of emergency and the banning of the African National Congress and the <b>Pan Africanist Congress</b>, and in December 1961 the ANC and the South African Communist Party launched an armed wing, Umkhonto we Sizwe, with Nelson Mandela as its first commander.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 21 March 1960 police at Sharpeville fired on a crowd protesting against the pass laws, killing 69 people; the government declared a state of emergency and banned the ANC and the <b>Pan Africanist Congress</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Africanists who founded the <b>Pan Africanist Congress</b> (PAC) under Robert Sobukwe in 1959 held that Africans must lead their own liberation and distrusted the influence of white communists in the Congress Alliance.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
